{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "ואם עד אלה", :translit "v'im ad eileh", :gloss "and if despite these things"} {:he "ס\"ת", :translit "sofei teivot", :gloss "final letters"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :sofei-tevot, :phrase "ואם עד אלה", :spells "סת"}], :frame {:text "The concept of midah keneged midah — measure for measure — is a foundational principle of divine justice. The phrase 'middah keneged middah' as a technical expression appears in multiple rabbinic discussions of divine punishment and reward (Sotah 8b–9a, where it is derived from verses in Deut. 19:19 and others). The specific phrase 'middah she-omedet' is not itself a direct rabbinic quotation. Ba'al HaTurim reads the final letters of 'ואם עד אלה' as spelling סת, linking the verse's warning about cumulative punishment to the concept of measured divine justice.", :sources ["Sotah 8b"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :sofei-tevot, :phrase "ואם עד אלה", :spells "סת", :letters ["ם" "ד" "ה"], :by :selah, :order :mismatch}], :book :leviticus, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:26:14.046406561Z", :model "glm-5.2"}